<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Max Gluckman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Max Gluckman was a South African-born British social anthropologist, director of the Rhodes-Livingstone Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> and founder of the Manchester School of anthropology. His study The Judicial Process among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Barotse</a> of Northern Rhodesia (1955) showed that Lozi courts reasoned with the standard of the reasonable man, and his work on conflict and ritual shaped the anthropology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p></div>
